# REPL options and resource budgets.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# Everything configurable in the REPL is an option. get with no argument lists
# them all with their current values; get with a name reads a single one:

get
get charvar

# The boolean options are switched with set, enable, disable or toggle:

toggle colors
get colors
toggle colors
disable status
enable status

# severity picks how much the REPL reports (error/info/debug/trace); note the
# prompt announcing the debug level while it is active:

set severity debug
set severity info

# charvar is worth a closer look, because it changes what a variable IS. With
# charvar on -- the default -- a variable is a single letter plus digits, so
# xyzvw below is a product of five variables (juxtaposition is conjunction,
# demo_1.1), and normalization orders them:

n xyzvw

# With charvar off, a variable is any word: the same input is now one variable
# named xyzvw, and descriptive names become possible:

set charvar off
n xyzvw
n count' | count
set charvar on

# The numeric options in the get listing are budgets. Each caps one of the
# engine's iterative searches; 0 means unlimited, which is the default for all
# but the two gc knobs. They exist because several procedures search rather
# than compute: a search that does not converge is better cut off loudly than
# left running. Reaching a budget is a bound on the search, never an answer
# about the input. Every budget has a command line twin (--max-fixpoint-steps,
# --max-enum-steps, ... -- see the README).

# fixpointsteps caps the temporal-normalization fixpoint search behind the
# decision procedures of demo_1.5. A specification with lookback 2 needs at
# least 2 steps, so a cap of 1 makes that phase give up with an error -- here
# another phase of the sat procedure still decides the query afterwards:

set fixpointsteps 1
sat always o1[t] = o1[t-2]
set fixpointsteps 0

# enumsteps caps the fixpoint enumeration of recurrence relations (demo_1.3).
# The function below alternates 0, 1, 0, ... forever; its loop is only
# recognized at the third enumerated step, so a budget of 2 cuts the search
# off before the loop (and the fallback answer) can be found -- reaching a
# budget bounds the search, it does not answer for the input:

b[0](x):sbf := 0
b[n](x):sbf := b[n-1](x)'
set enumsteps 2
n b(y:sbf) fallback last

# With the budget lifted, the same call detects the loop and returns the
# fallback:

set enumsteps 0
n b(y:sbf) fallback last

# defpasses caps definition expansion. The pair below re-creates g2 with
# every expansion pass, so it can never settle; the budget bounds how many
# passes are attempted before reporting. (Uncapped, an oscillation detector
# catches this particular pair too -- the budget is for the searches nothing
# detects.)

set defpasses 1
g2(x):sbf := h2(g2(x))
h2(x):sbf := x'
n g2(0:sbf)
set defpasses 0

# The remaining budgets follow the same pattern on other searches: maxsplits,
# maxrounds, blastdepth and squeezecap bound anti-prenexing, maxclauses bounds
# complete quantifier elimination, simplifyrounds and rewriterounds bound
# rewriting, flagsteps bounds the eventual-flag search of satisfiability, and
# specsizewarn / revisionalts guard the updatable runs of demo_3.5. gcminsize
# and gcgrowth tune the interpreter's tree garbage collector and keep their
# defaults unless set.

quit
